# Work only on AIC repositories (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-27), verbatim:

> **ALWAYS: Work only on my repos. unless told otherwise. Do not modify
> Robert's repos.**

## The rule

> **Every edit, stage, commit, and push goes to an Analog Informatics
> Corporation (AIC) repository. Robert Jones's repositories are read-only
> from AIC work. Do not edit, stage, commit, push, or force-push them, on
> any branch, even when a change there would be convenient. The only
> exception is an explicit operator instruction naming that repository in
> the same message.**

## Robert-owned: read-only

| Repository | Posture |
| --- | --- |
| `mix-server` | **Read-only.** No commits, no pushes, no branches, on any branch |
| `mix-orm` | **Read-only.** Same |
| Any other repository the operator identifies as Robert's | **Read-only** |

Reading them is encouraged. [`reuse-robert-core-first.mdc`](reuse-robert-core-first.mdc)
requires searching that code before writing new domain logic, and reading
costs nothing. What is forbidden is writing.

When AIC needs a change in locked upstream code, the path is a fork under
an AIC-owned name, per
[`fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc`](fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc). Never a
direct edit, and never an uncommitted local change sitting in his tree.

When AIC work should eventually reach Mix, it is **offered** through a
merge-back note and Robert merges on his own process. The Operational
Technology program is the worked example:
[`pam-platform-docs/docs/ot/ot-mix-server-merge-back.md`](../../pam-platform-docs/docs/ot/ot-mix-server-merge-back.md).

## AIC-owned: normal work

The repositories under `github.com/analoginfo-pim` that AIC develops in,
including `aic-server`, `aic-orm`, `pam-platform-docs`, `core-assets`,
`operator-workflows`, the `pim-*` family, the `pam-*` family, and
`pim-installers`. The repository map is
[`pam-platform-docs/docs/REPO_INDEX.md`](../../pam-platform-docs/docs/REPO_INDEX.md).

Normal commit and push rules apply there
([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)), including explicit-path
staging ([`shared-working-tree-is-not-yours.mdc`](shared-working-tree-is-not-yours.mdc)).

## Beyond git: the same boundary applies outward

"Work only on my repos" also rules out editing anything the operator does
not own:

- Do not modify installed packages, virtual environments, or site-packages
  to work around a bug. Fix our code, or pin a version.
- Do not edit another vendor's configuration or files on the host outside
  our own trees ([`host-session-safety.mdc`](host-session-safety.mdc)).
- Do not attempt changes to public or third-party repositories. Harvest
  ideas from public sources and implement them in our own trees instead.

Installing a normal developer tool, such as a linter, is not a repository
change and stays allowed.

## Forbidden constructs

- Any `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout -b`, or branch
  creation inside `mix-server` or `mix-orm`.
- Leaving uncommitted edits in a Robert-owned working tree, which is worse
  than a commit because it is invisible and unattributed.
- Editing a Robert-owned file "temporarily" to unblock a local build. Report
  the exact path and error to the operator and continue on what you can.
- Committing a file in an AIC repository that a concurrent agent is still
  editing. Check `git status` and stage only your own paths.
- Modifying installed third-party packages in place.
- Opening a change against a public repository.

## Required agent behavior

1. Before the first edit in a session, know which repository each target
   path belongs to. If a path sits under `mix-server` or `mix-orm`, stop.
2. If a build or test fails because of code in a Robert-owned repository,
   report the path and the error. Do not fix it there.
3. If the work genuinely requires an upstream change, follow
   [`fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc`](fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc) and push
   the fork to an AIC-owned remote.
4. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change set.
   The workspace `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation.** An AIC feature needs a field added to a Mix ORM model. The
agent edits `mix-orm/src/model.rs`, builds locally, and moves on. The change
is never committed, so it is invisible to Robert and to every other agent,
and the next person to pull his repository silently loses it or hits a
conflict they cannot explain.

**Fix.** Leave `mix-orm` untouched. Fork to an AIC-owned repository, make
the change there, push it, pin the consumer at that git revision, and record
what Robert may merge later in a merge-back note.

